/**
 * PROSTATE CANCER: LIVING WITH IT, CARE AND DECISIONS (UK and NHS focus). Facts checked 25 September 2026.
 *
 * The patient side of the prostate record, written to the lung and colorectal standard (./lung-living.ts,
 * ./colorectal-living.ts): eight decision rows, each of which the decisions page turns into a section (the central
 * choice in localised disease between active surveillance, surgery and radiotherapy, with the trial's own figures
 * for what each does to continence and erections; what radiotherapy actually involves and why hormone therapy comes
 * with it; living with androgen deprivation, which is the longest and least covered part of this illness; continence
 * and sexual function and the services that exist for both; a rising PSA after treatment; cancer that has reached
 * the bone, with spinal cord compression named as the emergency it is; a trial or standard treatment; and when to
 * talk about palliative care). It also carries the hand-written question sets the appointment sheet, prep pack and
 * decision pages read (exported as `prostateQuestions`, wired in src/data/questions.ts), and living-with material
 * attached as sourced notes on the records other files own.
 *
 * On tone. The reader is a man who was told this week, or someone who loves him. He is often not old: prostate
 * cancer is diagnosed at every age from the forties on, and the ProtecT trial that most of these figures come from
 * recruited men between 50 and 69. Nothing here is coy. Incontinence is called leaking, impotence is called not
 * being able to get an erection, and both are given their numbers, because the alternative, a page that says
 * "side effects" and moves on, is how men end up unprepared for the thing that actually happens to them. Every
 * number carries its cohort, and nothing here is a prognosis for an individual.
 *
 * This file creates no glossary terms: the glossary pass (agent A) owns them. Drafts for the terms this material
 * wants are exported as `prostateLivingTermDrafts`, written to the glossary standard so they can be registered by
 * spreading the array; the ids they use are referenced nowhere in the graph until then. Trials are owned by the
 * trials pass (agent B): no trial record is created here, and the rows reference only trials the corpus already
 * has (`protect`, `stampede`, `embark`).
 *
 * Sources, all read 25 September 2026: NICE NG131 (prostate cancer: diagnosis and management, May 2019, last
 * updated 15 December 2021) and NICE NG234 (spinal metastases and metastatic spinal cord compression, 2023); the
 * NHS prostate cancer, erection problems, priapism, sepsis, clinical trials and carers pages; Prostate Cancer UK's
 * living-with, hormone therapy, hot flushes, sex and relationships, urinary problems, pelvic floor, fatigue and
 * metastatic spinal cord compression pages; Macmillan; Cancer Research UK; Marie Curie; Maggie's; and the papers
 * Hamdy et al. (NEJM 2023), Donovan et al. (NEJM 2016 and NEJM Evidence 2023), Shahinian et al. (NEJM 2005),
 * Keating et al. (JCO 2006), Shore et al. (NEJM 2020), Irani et al. (Lancet Oncology 2010) and Nead et al.
 * (Urologic Oncology 2017). Nothing here is advice for an individual and every page says so.
 */

